<%# One flash, rendered by every layout.

    There used to be three copies. The application and devise layouts carried
    identical markup; the Player's was a bare div with the flash and variant
    classes interpolated straight from the flash type, and no .flash__body —
    which is where the fill, padding and radius live — so a Player flash
    rendered as unstyled ink text floating over the page. Three copies is how
    that happens, and the Player is the surface agents live in.

    Iterating flash rather than naming notice and alert means a flash type
    nobody anticipated still renders as a message instead of vanishing.

    Careful with ERB comments here: an example containing a closing tag ends the
    comment early and dumps the remainder of it into the page as literal text. %>
<% flash.each do |type, message| %>
  <% next if message.blank? %>
  <%= render "shared/flash_message", type:, message: %>
<% end %>
